==Controversy==
In March 2017, Arunabh Kumar found himself in the middle of a sexual harassment claim that was posted anonymously via [[Medium (website)|Medium]]. Several women have come out with similar stories of harassment. TVF released a press release via Medium refuting the claims.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thehindu.com/news/national/reports-of-more-harrassment-at-the-viral-fever/article17460810.ece|title=More reports of sexual harassment surfaced at 'The Viral Fever'|last=Purkayastha|first=Debasree|work=The Hindu|access-date=2017-03-15|language=en}}</ref>

Mumbai's [[Versova, Mumbai|Versova]] Police registered a second FIR against Kumar after another woman filed a complaint against him over an incident she said took place in 2014.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://scroll.in/latest/833308/second-fir-lodged-against-tvf-chief-arunabh-kumar-for-sexual-harassment|title=Second FIR lodged against TVF chief Arunabh Kumar for sexual harassment|work=NewsBytes|access-date=2017-03-31|language=en}}</ref> On 16 June 2017, Kumar stepped down as TVF CEO. Dhawal Gusain now leads the company as CEO, with Karan Chaudhry stepping in as COO and Sameer Saxena, the creator of ''Permanent Roommates'' & ''Tripling'', appointed as CCO.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thehindu.com/business/Industry/arunabh-kumar-steps-down-as-tvf-ceo/article19088518.ece|title=Arunabh Kumar steps down as TVF CEO|date=2017-06-16|work=The Hindu|access-date=2018-02-25|language=en-IN|issn=0971-751X}}</ref>

